\section{Conclusion}

We presented an episode-level counterfactual analysis methodology for diagnosing routing behavior in modular LLM agent systems. Applied to 245 episodes across 11 ScienceWorld task types, our analysis reveals that death avoidance accounts for 90.41\% of the routing signal between internal LLM reasoning and deterministic computation ($\Delta_\text{full}$ = +29.60 vs.\ $\Delta_\text{nd}$ = +2.84). This concentration is corroborated by a near-perfect correlation between per-task death rate differentials and routing benefit ($r$ = 0.9698, $p$ = 8.12$\times$10$^{-7}$), the oracle router's 96.2\% agreement with always-deterministic, and a trained router's near-random balanced accuracy of 58.1\%.

Our study has several limitations. The analysis uses a single benchmark (ScienceWorld) with a single LLM backbone (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct), and the $-100$ death penalty may amplify the measured DAF. Future work should extend to step-level counterfactual labeling for finer-grained diagnostics, evaluate generalization to environments with different failure modes (e.g., MATH, ALFWorld), and investigate whether stronger backbones exhibit richer non-death routing signals.

\begin{abstract}
Modular LLM agents that route between neural reasoning and deterministic computation promise improved reliability in rule-governed environments, yet understanding \emph{what} drives effective routing remains unclear. Per-step oracle labeling degenerates in sparse-reward settings---in ScienceWorld, 98\% of steps yield tied outcomes, rendering step-level analysis uninformative. We propose episode-level counterfactual analysis: running complete episodes under pure strategies and comparing final outcomes to derive per-episode routing labels. Applying this methodology to 245 episodes across 11 task types with a Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct backbone, we find that death avoidance accounts for 90.41\% of the routing signal (Death Avoidance Fraction). Per-task death rate differentials correlate near-perfectly with routing benefit ($r$ = 0.9698, $p$ = 8.12$\times$10$^{-7}$). The oracle router agrees with always-deterministic on 96.2\% of decisions, and a trained router achieves only 58.1\% balanced accuracy---near random. These results indicate that deterministic computation's value is concentrated in catastrophic failure prevention rather than positive task guidance.
\end{abstract}
